<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Svetitskhoveli Cathedral</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1029</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Svetitskhoveli Cathedral is the principal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mtskheta">Mtskheta</a>, the ancient capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, standing on the site of the first church built after the conversion of King Mirian III in the fourth century. The present building was completed around 1029 by the architect Arsukisdze, and it was long the coronation and burial church of the Georgian kings. It is part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/svetitskhoveli-cathedral.jpg" alt="Svetitskhoveli Cathedral" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This Photo was taken by Supanut Arunoprayote. Feel free to use any of my images, but please mention me as the author ... · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Svetitskhoveli_Cathedral_09.23_(3).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
